---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';
---

<Base title="About — Tom De Smedt" description="Tom De Smedt: hands-on tech leader building AI systems and the teams that ship them.">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>Hands-on tech leadership</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I'm Tom De Smedt, a Belgium-based technology leader. I've spent more than a decade
        leading tech, IT and data teams — from startups to scale-ups and international
        organisations — including several years managing highly effective engineering teams.
      </p>
      <p>
        These days most of my work is applied AI: retrieval-augmented generation, agentic
        systems, data products, and the unglamorous engineering that turns a demo into
        something a business can rely on. I work across the stack — strategy on Monday,
        code review on Tuesday — because I believe technical leaders should stay close
        enough to the work to know what's real.
      </p>
      <!-- TODO(Tom): confirm/adjust the paragraph below — certifications and domains claimed in your earlier posts. -->
      <p>
        Along the way I've been a Certified Scrum Master, led technology in domains ranging
        from food tech to life sciences, and built data science capabilities from scratch.
        The <a href="/blog/">archive</a> holds my earlier writing on leadership and data;
        the newer posts are field notes from building AI systems, each with runnable code
        in <a href="https://github.com/tdesmedt-tdsgn/labs">the labs repo</a>.
      </p>
      <h2>Why this blog exists</h2>
      <p>
        Too much AI writing is speculation. My rule here: <strong>if I write about it, I
        built it</strong> — and you can run it. Every technical post links to a working,
        tested demo, and every post ends with what it means in business terms, because the
        gap between those two conversations is exactly where projects fail.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Cta />
  </div>
</Base>
